'''Jocelyn Michelle Benson''' (born October 22, 1977)<ref>{{cite news|url = https://www.newspapers.com/image/1183694669/|title = On the ballot|newspaper = [[The Bay City Times]]|date = October 8, 2010|accessdate = September 29, 2025|page = A8|via = [[Newspapers.com]]|url-access = subscription}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.legistorm.com/person/bio/466060/Jocelyn_Michelle_Benson.html|title=Jocelyn Michelle Benson - Michigan Department of State (Jan. 2019-), Secretary of State - Biography|website=[[LegiStorm]]|publisher=LegiStorm|access-date=October 23, 2025}}</ref> is an American politician and attorney who has served as the 43rd [[Michigan Secretary of State|secretary of state of Michigan]] since 2019. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], she served as dean of [[Wayne State University Law School]] from 2012 to 2016.

In [[2018 Michigan Secretary of State election|2018]], Benson was elected as the secretary of state in Michigan, securing the position with an 8.9 percentage point margin over Republican Mary Treder Lang. This victory made her the first Democrat to hold the office since 1995. She was reelected in [[2022 Michigan Secretary of State election|2022]], defeating Republican [[Kristina Karamo]] by a margin of 14 percentage points, setting a record for the largest margin and vote share among statewide candidates that year.

Benson is the Democratic nominee in the [[2026 Michigan gubernatorial election]] and faces Congressman [[John James (Michigan politician)|John James]] in the general.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Smith |first=Mitch |date=2026-08-05 |title=Michigan Secretary of State Wins Democratic Nomination for Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/04/us/elections/michigan-democratic-primary-governor.html |access-date=2026-08-05 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> 

==Education and early career==
Benson was raised in [[Pittsburgh]], Pennsylvania.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.gongwer.com/directories/bio.cfm?nameid=295801 |title=Gongwer News Service-Michigan}}</ref><ref name=aid>{{cite news |last1=King |first1=Kurt Anthony |title=Coming to the aid |url=https://legalnews.com/Home/Articles?DataId=1368216 |access-date=5 March 2025 |work=legalnews.com |date=October 15, 2012}}</ref> She graduated from [[Wellesley College]] with a bachelor's degree in 1999. She received a master's in sociology as a [[Marshall Scholarship|Marshall Scholar]] at [[Magdalen College, Oxford]].<ref name=aid/> After college, Benson moved from Massachusetts to [[Montgomery, Alabama]], to work for the [[Southern Poverty Law Center]], where she investigated hate groups and hate crimes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Barnett |first1=Lana |title=The battle for the ballot box |url=https://hls.harvard.edu/today/the-battle-for-the-ballot-box/ |access-date=5 March 2025 |work=Harvard Law School |date=August 19, 2021 |language=en-us}}</ref> Benson also worked as a legal assistant to [[Nina Totenberg]] at [[National Public Radio]] (NPR).<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dbusiness.com/people/wayne-state-names-new-dean-of-law-school/ |title=Wayne State Names New Dean of Law School - DBusiness Magazine |publisher=Dbusiness.com |date= June 12, 2014|accessdate=2022-02-04}}</ref>

Benson received a [[Juris Doctor]] degree from [[Harvard Law School]], where she was a general editor of the [[Harvard Law Review|''Harvard Civil Rights-Civil Liberties Law Review'']].<ref>{{cite web |title=Archive |url=https://harvardcrcl.org/archive/ |website=Harvard Civil Rights-Civil Liberties Law Review |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210806103718/https://harvardcrcl.org/archive/ |archive-date=August 6, 2021}}</ref><ref name="wsu">{{cite web |url=http://www.law.wayne.edu/faculty/bio.php?id=42981 |title=Wayne State University Law School Bio for Jocelyn F. Benson |publisher=Law.wayne.edu |access-date=August 2, 2018 |archive-date=July 2, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100702222517/http://www.law.wayne.edu/faculty/bio.php?id=42981 |url-status=dead }}</ref> From 2002 to 2004, she was the voting rights policy coordinator of the [[Civil Rights Project]].<ref name="wsu" /> Upon graduation from law school, Benson moved to [[Detroit]], Michigan, to serve as a [[law clerk]] to Judge [[Damon Keith]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit]].<ref>{{cite web|author=Wayne State University |url=https://law.wayne.edu/news/wayne-law-remembers-judge-damon-j-keith-33919 |title=Wayne Law remembers Judge Damon J. Keith - Law School - Wayne State University |publisher=Law.wayne.edu |date= July 23, 2019|accessdate=2022-02-04}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://miwf.org/timeline/jocelyn-benson/ |title=Jocelyn Benson - Michigan Women Forward |publisher=Miwf.org |date= |accessdate=2022-02-04}}</ref>

Benson became a faculty member of the [[Wayne State University Law School]] in Detroit in 2005.<ref name="freepress" /> In 2010, Benson published a book, ''State Secretaries of State: Guardians of the Democratic Process''.<ref>{{cite book |last1=Benson |first1=Jocelyn F. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_-D6PfWpQtgC&q=state+secretaries+of+state+benson&pg=PA1 |title=State Secretaries of State: Guardians of the Democratic Process |date=28 February 2013 |publisher=Ashgate Publishing |isbn=978-1-4094-9699-1 |language=en}}</ref> In 2012, at the age of 36, Benson was appointed dean of the Wayne State University Law School, becoming the youngest woman ever to lead an accredited law school.<ref name="freepress">{{cite news |last1=Jesse |first1=David |title=Jocelyn Benson to step down as dean of Wayne State Law School |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/detroit/2016/09/06/jocelyn-benson-dean-wayne-state-law-school/89911160/ |access-date=5 March 2025 |work=Detroit Free Press |date=September 6, 2016}}</ref>

In 2016, Benson stepped down as dean of the Wayne State University Law School to serve as CEO of the Ross Initiative in Sports for Equality, a New York-based group funded by [[Stephen M. Ross]].<ref name="freepress" /> She served in this position until 2018.<ref>{{cite web|date=2019-03-17|title=Benson drew salary from RISE while seeking office|url=https://www.crainsdetroit.com/government/benson-drew-salary-rise-while-seeking-office|access-date=2020-11-11|website=Crain's Detroit Business|language=en}}</ref>

==Secretary of State==
[[File:P20230106AS-1509 (52644320902).jpg|thumb|right|Benson receives the [[Presidential Citizens Medal]] from President [[Joe Biden]] in January 2023.]]

===Elections===
In 2010, while a faculty member at Wayne Law, Benson mounted her first campaign for [[Michigan Secretary of State]].<ref>{{cite web |first=Ryan J. |last=Stanton |date=2010-09-17 |title=Secretary of state candidate Jocelyn Benson calls on help from University of Michigan students |url=http://www.annarbor.com/news/secretary-of-state-candidate-jocelyn-benson-calls-on-help-from-university-of-michigan-students/ |accessdate=2022-02-04 |website=The Ann Arbor News}}</ref> She lost to Republican candidate [[Ruth Johnson]] by a margin of 51 percent to 45 percent.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wisely |first=John |title=Jocelyn Benson wins race for Michigan Secretary of State |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2018/11/06/michigan-secretary-state-results-benson-treder-lang/1844392002/ |access-date=2023-10-18 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref>

On October 27, 2017, Benson announced her second candidacy for Michigan Secretary of State. She was elected on November 6, 2018, defeating [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] Mary Treder Lang, becoming the first Democrat to serve since [[Richard H. Austin|Richard Austin]] left office in 1995.<ref>{{cite web |title=2018 Michigan Official General Election Results - 11/06/2018 |url=https://mielections.us/election/results/2018GEN_CENR.html |access-date=2019-02-24 |website=mielections.us |archive-date=January 4, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200104202715/https://mielections.us/election/results/2018GEN_CENR.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> In 2022, Benson secured her party's nomination uncontested and went on to win the general election with 56% of the vote, defeating Republican candidate [[Kristina Karamo]].<ref name=":02">{{Cite web |title=Jocelyn Benson faces Kristina Karamo in Michigan secretary of state race: What to know |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/10/20/meet-the-candidates-in-michigan-secretary-of-state-race/69561951007/ |access-date=2023-10-18 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Jocelyn Benson beats Kristina Karamo in Michigan's Secretary of State race |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/11/09/jocelyn-benson-wins-2022-michigan-secretary-of-state-race/69599440007/ |access-date=2023-10-18 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Tenure===
In 2018, Michigan voters approved a [[constitutional amendment]] allowing [[Election Day (United States)|Election Day]] voter registration and unrestricted [[Absentee ballot|absentee ballots]]. Benson's office oversaw the implementation of these changes.<ref name=":03">{{Cite web |title=Jocelyn Benson beats Kristina Karamo in Michigan's Secretary of State race |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/11/09/jocelyn-benson-wins-2022-michigan-secretary-of-state-race/69599440007/ |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref> In 2019, the Election Modernization Advisory Committee was established.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dunaj |first=Mikhayla |date=December 25, 2020 |title=No-reason absentee voting in Michigan: How it worked, how it can be improved |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/2020/12/25/no-reason-absentee-voter-education-2020-election/3969991001/ |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref> The same year, Benson established an Election Security Commission<ref>{{Cite web |last=Manes |first=Nick |date=March 21, 2019 |title=Benson forms election security commission |url=https://michiganadvance.com/blog/benson-forms-election-security-commission/ |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=Michigan Advance |language=en-US}}</ref> and a Collegiate Student Advisory Task Force.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Donahue |first=Allison R. |date=2020-03-07 |title=Young people gear up for the 2020 election, whether they can vote or not|url=https://michiganadvance.com/2020/03/07/young-people-gear-up-for-the-2020-election-whether-they-can-vote-or-not/ |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=Michigan Advance |language=en-US}}</ref>

In October 2019, Benson was named in a lawsuit filed by [[Priorities USA Action]], a Democratic group, over the elimination of absentee votes due to disparities between a voter's signature and other filed documents with election officials. In November, the group targeted her in a lawsuit challenging the state's automatic registration law and alleging undue burdens on young voters' constitutional rights.<ref>{{Cite web |last=LeBlanc |first=Beth |title=Dem group files third voting rights lawsuit, this one targeting Benson |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/michigan/2019/11/15/dem-group-files-third-voting-rights-lawsuit-one-targeting-benson/4205028002/ |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=The Detroit News |language=en-US}}</ref> In March 2021, Michigan's Court of Claims ruled against Benson, saying that although the instructions' content did not breach election law, she had not followed the appropriate procedural measures while providing guidance to clerks on verifying signatures on absentee ballots.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2023-10-25 |title=Michigan Appeals Court rules that secretary of state improperly set limits for poll challengers |url=https://www.votebeat.org/2023/10/25/jocelyn-benson-poll-challenger-rules-republicans-lawsuit/ |access-date=2023-11-02 |website=Votebeat |language=en}}</ref>

In 2020, Benson's office mailed absentee voter applications to all 7.7 million Michigan registered voters for the August primary and November general election. Benson's effort was funded by $4.5 million in federal coronavirus relief funding from the [[CARES Act]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Beggin |first=Riley |title=Jocelyn Benson orders Michigan to mail absentee applications to all voters {{!}} Bridge Michigan |url=https://www.bridgemi.com/michigan-government/jocelyn-benson-orders-michigan-mail-absentee-applications-all-voters |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=Bridge Michigan |date=May 19, 2020 |language=en}}</ref> Some Republican lawmakers criticized the move, saying it could endanger election security by leaving more room for voter fraud.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gibbons |first=Lauren |date=July 20, 2022 |title=Michigan absentee applications nearly double from 2018: 1.2 million so far {{!}} Bridge Michigan |url=https://www.bridgemi.com/michigan-government/michigan-absentee-applications-nearly-double-2018-12-million-so-far |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=Bridge Michigan |language=en}}</ref> Benson's predecessor, [[Ruth Johnson]], criticized her for using COVID-19 relief funds to mail absentee ballot applications to all registered voters.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Burke |first=Beth LeBlanc and Melissa Nann |title=All state registered voters to be mailed Aug., Nov. absentee ballot applications |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/michigan/2020/05/19/all-state-registered-voters-mailed-august-november-absentee-ballot-applications/5219068002/ |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=The Detroit News |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-05-19 |title=Michigan mails absentee ballot applications to all voters |url=https://apnews.com/article/5ce654fb8df4d29be57b593055cb99a6 |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref>

As President Trump voiced concerns about potential election fraud associated with absentee voting, his criticism particularly targeted Benson's mailing effort. He called Benson a "rogue Secretary of State"<ref name=":42">{{Cite web |last=Beggin |first=Riley |title=Michigan GOP lawmakers claim Jocelyn Benson's absentee ballot mailings illegal {{!}} Bridge Michigan |url=https://www.bridgemi.com/michigan-government/michigan-gop-lawmakers-claim-jocelyn-bensons-absentee-ballot-mailings-illegal |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=Bridge Michigan |date=June 24, 2020 |language=en}}</ref> and initially threatened to withhold federal funds for coronavirus relief, but later retracted his threat.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Gray |first=Kathleen |date=2020-07-12 |title=Trump vs. the Women Who Lead Michigan: A Battle With 2020 Implications |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/12/us/politics/trump-michigan-whitmer-benson-nessel.html |access-date=2023-10-15 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name=":03"/> Court rulings affirmed Benson's legal authority to mail absentee ballot applications to all registered voters in Michigan in 2020, while the state auditor discredited Republican claims of fraudulent ballots from deceased voters.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mauger |first=Craig |title=New Michigan audit debunks dead voter theory in 2020 election |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2022/03/04/audit-debunks-dead-voter-theory-michigan-2020-election/9373387002/ |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=The Detroit News |language=en-US}}</ref> On December 7, 2020, reportedly armed [[pro-Trump]] demonstrators gathered outside Benson's home, echoing Trump's election fraud claims. The demonstrators chanted and made threatening demands to overturn Michigan's election results, linking the incident to broader efforts by Trump and his legal team to challenge the election outcome. No arrests were made and police confirmed that there were no breaches of criminal law throughout the demonstration.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2020-12-07 |title=Jocelyn Benson: Armed protesters flock to Michigan official's home |language=en-GB |work=BBC News |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/election-us-2020-55220570 |access-date=2023-10-15}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |first1=Annie |last1=Grayer |first2=Scottie |last2=Andrew |date=2020-12-07 |title=Michigan secretary of state says armed protesters gathered outside her home, claiming voter fraud |url=https://www.cnn.com/2020/12/07/us/michigan-secretary-of-state-armed-protesters-trnd/index.html |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref>

In January 2020, a 72-year-old man was arrested after evidence was found in his home related to violent threats he directed at Benson.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ramirez |first=Charles E. |title=Man, 72, arrested for making threats against Michigan's secretary of state |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/oakland-county/2020/01/30/man-72-arrested-making-threats-against-michigans-secretary-state/4619077002/ |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=The Detroit News |language=en-US}}</ref> In December 2022, Benson said that threats from election deniers that started in 2020 had not ceased.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-12-02 |title=Michigan Secretary of State Jocelyn Benson still facing threats stemming from 2020 election — "The Takeout" |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/michigan-secretary-of-state-jocelyn-benson-threats-2020-election-the-takeout/ |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=CBS |language=en-US}}</ref> In May 2023, she claimed in an interview that after the election, Trump suggested she be tried for treason and possibly executed, an accusation he denied.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Cynthia |last1=McFadden |first2=Kevin |last2=Monahan |first3=Alexandra |last3=Chaidez |date=2022-05-19 |title=Michigan election chief: Trump suggested I be arrested for treason and executed |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2022/05/19/michigan-election-chief-trump-suggested-i-be-arrested-for-treason-and-executed.html |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=CNBC |language=en}}</ref>

In June 2020, Benson initiated a platform enabling registered voters to apply for an absentee ballot online, using their [[Identity documents in the United States|state ID]] and last four digits of their [[Social Security number|Social Security numbers]]. Voters were also allowed to submit a scanned, signed copy of their absentee ballot application via email.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Beggin |first=Riley |title=Michigan GOP lawmakers claim Jocelyn Benson's absentee ballot mailings illegal {{!}} Bridge Michigan |url=https://www.bridgemi.com/michigan-government/michigan-gop-lawmakers-claim-jocelyn-bensons-absentee-ballot-mailings-illegal |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=Bridge Michigan |date=June 24, 2020 |language=en}}</ref> Benson also played a role in developing an online ballot-tracking tool for absentee ballots.<ref name=":22">{{Cite web |last=Rich |first=Samantha |date=2022-11-09 |title=Jocelyn Benson wins re-election bid as Michigan Secretary of State |url=http://www.michigandaily.com/news/secretary-of-state-jocelyn-benson-reelected-for-second-term/ |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=The Michigan Daily |language=en-US}}</ref> Benson said that the attempts to [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|delegitimize the 2020 election]] ultimately resulted in the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Weykamp |first=George |date=2021-09-14 |title=Jocelyn Benson speaks at UMich Law School about voter rights |url=http://www.michigandaily.com/government/michigan-secretary-of-state-jocelyn-benson-speaks-about-2020-election-protecting-voting-rights-at-law-school-event/ |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=The Michigan Daily |language=en-US}}</ref> In March 2021, State Court of Claims Judge [[Christopher M. Murray]] ruled that Benson's guidance issued to Michigan clerks prior to the 2020 election, instructing them to presume the accuracy of absentee ballot signatures, was invalid because it had not gone through the proper rule-making process.<ref>{{cite web |title=Judge rules Secretary of State Benson's Ballot signature verification invalid - 03/15/2021 |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2021/03/15/judge-rules-secretary-state-bensons-ballot-signature-verification-guidance-invalid/4699927001/ |access-date=2022-09-19 |website=thedetroitnews.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Fact Checks |url=https://www.michigan.gov/sos/faqs/elections-and-campaign-finance/fact-checks |access-date=2022-09-26 |website=michigan.gov |language=en}}</ref>

Referencing Michigan's 2015 ranking as the lowest in the U.S. for ethics and transparency, Benson has advocated for ethics reforms and called for increased transparency in the state's government in the wake of various scandals.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nichols |first=Anna Liz |date=2023-06-22 |title=Benson asks Legislature to make Michigan 'worst to first' for government transparency  |url=https://michiganadvance.com/2023/06/22/benson-asks-legislature-to-make-michigan-worst-to-first-for-government-transparency/ |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=Michigan Advance |language=en-US}}</ref> In March 2021, Benson introduced a legislative agenda called "From Worst to First" which included proposed measures such as expanding the [[Freedom of Information Act (United States)|Freedom of Information Act]] to cover the [[Governor of Michigan|governor]] and legislature, requiring personal [[Financial disclosure of public servants|financial disclosures]] from elected officials, demanding more frequent campaign disclosures, and tightening campaign finance regulations.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hendrickson |first=Clara |title=Michigan Secretary of State Jocelyn Benson unveils plan to improve government transparency |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/detroit/2021/03/15/benson-michigan-government-transparency/4699440001/ |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref> In 2023, she criticized inaction from legislators, describing the state's [[lobbying]] and [[Campaign finance in the United States|campaign finance]] laws as "far, far behind" other states.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Robinson |first=Samuel |date=May 5, 2023 |title=Benson calls for transparency reforms — will Democrats listen? |url=https://www.axios.com/local/detroit/2023/05/05/michigan-sos-jocelyn-benson-transparency-reforms |website=Axios}}</ref>

In April 2021, Benson announced that Michigan residents who visit secretary of state offices would always need an appointment.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-04-30 |title=Appointment-only visits to secretary of state here to stay |url=https://apnews.com/article/health-coronavirus-government-and-politics-895699564ad6e44f2fce278327124f52 |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> That same year, she said 60% of transactions were being done virtually.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rahal |first=Sarah |title=Benson says 60% of SOS transactions are done without visiting branch offices |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/detroit-city/2021/12/14/benson-says-60-sos-transactions-can-done-without-visiting-braches/6501538001/ |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=The Detroit News |language=en-US}}</ref> Despite criticisms calling for the reintroduction of walk-in services, Benson defended retaining the system even after the [[COVID-19 pandemic]], saying the appointment only system is "not perfect" but is better than residents having to potentially wait hours in line.<ref>{{Cite web |first=David |last=Eggert |agency=The Associated Press |date=2021-05-30 |title=Benson defends appointment system amid legislative scrutiny |url=https://wwmt.com/news/state/benson-defends-appointment-system-amid-legislative-scrutiny-05-30-2021 |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=WWMT |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-05-30 |title=Benson defends appointment system amid legislative scrutiny |url=https://apnews.com/article/health-coronavirus-pandemic-government-and-politics-18bedb044b5c2035272f0c8800813ff2 |access-date=2023-10-15 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> In 2022, Benson was named as one of five recipients of the [[John F. Kennedy Profile in Courage Award]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Egan |first1=Paul |title="Secretary of State Jocelyn Benson honored with JFK Profile in Courage award" |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/elections/2022/04/21/michigan-jocelyn-benson-jfk-profile-courage-award/7393682001/ |website=Detroit Free Press |access-date=21 April 2022}}</ref> In September 2022, the Election Integrity Force and others sued Benson and Michigan's governor, [[Gretchen Whitmer]], in a bid to decertify the 2020 election.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Berzon |first1=Alexandra |last2=Bensinger |first2=Ken |date=2022-11-08 |title=Fueled by Falsehoods, a Michigan Group Is Ready to Challenge the Vote |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/11/07/us/politics/michigan-elections-group.html |access-date=2023-10-15 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> On January 6, 2023, Benson was awarded the [[Presidential Citizens Medal]] by President [[Joe Biden]].<ref>{{cite web | url=https://www.mlive.com/public-interest/2023/01/a-true-leader-michigan-sos-jocelyn-benson-receives-presidential-medal.html | title='A true leader,' Michigan SOS Jocelyn Benson receives presidential medal | date=January 6, 2023 }}</ref>

In July 2023, she confirmed that federal prosecutors interviewed her for "several hours" in March as part of the ongoing criminal probe into efforts to overturn the 2020 election. While not divulging specific details, Benson highlighted the investigators' focus on the impact on election workers' lives of misinformation about their work and threats of violence against them.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Cohen |first=Zachary |date=2023-07-12 |title=Federal prosecutors interviewed Michigan secretary of state in special counsel's election interference probe {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/07/12/politics/michigan-secretary-of-state-benson-interview-special-counsel/index.html |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref> In October 2023, a 60-year-old man from Detroit was sentenced to 15 months of probation after threatening to kill Benson and Michigan Governor [[Gretchen Whitmer]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Detroit man who threatened to kill Whitmer, Benson sentenced to 15 months probation |url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/local/michigan/detroit/2023/10/25/james-toepler-sentencing-whitmer-benson-threat/71317155007/ |access-date=2023-10-27 |website=Detroit Free Press |language=en-US}}</ref>

In a 2023 opinion piece for ''[[The Washington Post]]'', Benson argued that secretaries of state, including herself, shouldn't unilaterally decide Trump's eligibility to serve as President under the 14th Amendment of the U.S. Constitution, and emphasized that the courts, particularly the [[Supreme Court of the United States|Supreme Court]], are the appropriate forums for such determinations.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Benson |first=Jocelyn |date=September 13, 2023 |title=Opinion It's not up to secretaries of state like me to keep Trump off the ballot |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/opinions/2023/09/13/secretaries-of-state-trump-disqualification/ |newspaper=The Washington Post}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=SOS Benson says Trump will be on Michigan ballot unless court rules otherwise |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/2023/09/13/donald-trump-michigan-presidential-primary-ballot-insurrection-14th-amendment-jocelyn-benson/70843290007/ |access-date=2023-11-05 |website=The Detroit News |language=en-US}}</ref> In November 2023, Trump's legal team sued to prevent Benson from excluding him in Michigan's [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 elections]]. The lawsuit sought an injunction against barring him from the ballot. The dispute traces back to a [[Free Speech for People]] lawsuit, asserting that Trump's Capitol riot involvement violates Section 3 of the 14th Amendment.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Irwin |first=Lauren |date=2023-11-01 |title=Trump sues over efforts to keep him off Michigan ballot |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/4287173-trump-sues-over-efforts-to-keep-him-of-michigan-ballot/ |access-date=2023-11-05 |website=The Hill |language=en-US}}</ref>

In advance of the [[2024 United States presidential election|2024 presidential election]], Benson, along with other Democrats, participated in the Democracy Playbook, a set of recommendations by the NewDEAL Forum to improve the election system.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Coleman |first=Ken |date=February 20, 2023 |title=Benson works on national voting reforms, including big penalties for threatening election workers |url=https://michiganadvance.com/blog/benson-works-on-national-voting-reforms-including-big-penalties-for-threatening-elections-workers/ |access-date=2023-10-16 |website=Michigan Advance |language=en-US}}</ref> Benson supports stricter penalties against those who harass election workers and increased funding for election administration.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gibbons |first=Lauren |date=January 17, 2023 |title=Jocelyn Benson wants tougher penalties for harassing election workers |url=https://www.bridgemi.com/michigan-government/jocelyn-benson-wants-tougher-penalties-harassing-election-workers |website=Bridge Michigan}}</ref> Benson was viewed as a potential contender for the 2024 U.S. Senate race in Michigan to succeed retiring Senator [[Debbie Stabenow]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Manchester |first1=Julia |title=Stabenow retirement scrambles calculus for Michigan Democrats |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3803020-stabenow-retirement-scrambles-calculus-for-michigan-democrats/ |work=The Hill |date=January 8, 2023 |access-date=28 March 2023}}</ref> In March 2023, Benson announced that she would not enter the race.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Burke |first1=Melissa |title=Jocelyn Benson won't run for U.S. Senate in Michigan in 2024 |url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/politics/michigan/2023/03/24/benson-wont-run-for-u-s-senate-in-michigan-in-2024/70045879007/ |publisher=Detroit News |access-date=28 March 2023}}</ref>

== 2026 gubernatorial campaign ==
{{main|2026 Michigan gubernatorial election}}
In January 2025, on the anniversary of the U.S. Supreme Court's ''[[Roe v. Wade]]'' decision, she announced her candidacy for  Michigan governor in the [[2026 Michigan gubernatorial election|2026 race]] to succeed incumbent [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] governor [[Gretchen Whitmer]], who is term-limited and ineligible to run again.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Glueck |first=Katie |date=2025-01-22 |title=Jocelyn Benson, Michigan's Secretary of State, is Running for Governor |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/01/22/us/politics/jocelyn-benson-michigan-governor.html |access-date=2025-01-25 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> Her campaign was endorsed by [[United Auto Workers]] in June 2026, alongside [[Abdul El-Sayed]]'s campaign in the [[2026 United States Senate election in Michigan|2026 U.S. Senate election]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Lengel|first=Allan|date=2026-06-06|title=UAW endorses Democrat Joselyn Benson for Governor|newspaper=Deadline Detroit|url=https://deadlinedetroit.com/articles/34663/uaw_endorses_democrat_jocelyn_benson_for_governor|access-date=2026-06-06}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Benson married Ryan Friedrichs, whom she met while they were both students at Harvard, in 2006.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 15, 2014|title=Harvard grad wraps up military service in 173rd Airborne|url=https://www.dvidshub.net/news/134628/harvard-grad-wraps-up-military-service-173rd-airborne|access-date=2025-11-21|website=Defense Visual Information Distribution Service|language=en}}</ref> Friedrichs is a U.S. Army veteran and Benson has written about the isolation she experienced during his enlistment.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dickson|first=James David|title='Now, people get it': Bikers, runners join Veterans Day Parade in Corktown|url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/news/local/detroit-city/2019/11/10/now-people-get-it-bikers-runners-join-veterans-day-parade-corktown/2554978001/|access-date=2025-11-27|website=The Detroit News|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hendrickson|first=Clara|title=Michigan SOS Jocelyn Benson talks Trump, workplace harassment, miscarriage in new book|url=https://www.freep.com/story/news/politics/2025/05/06/michigan-sos-jocelyn-benson-book-tackles-trump-and-more/83458843007/|access-date=2025-11-21|website=Detroit Free Press|language=en-US}}</ref> In 2012, she founded Military Spouses of Michigan to provide support and build a network for military families in the state, noting the lack of an active duty base in Michigan. Benson and Friedrichs have one son.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 12, 2012|title=Coming to the aid|url=https://legalnews.com/Home/Articles?DataId=1368155|access-date=2025-11-21|website=Legal News}}</ref>

Friedrichs is a vice president for [[Related Companies]], a New York-based real estate firm owned by billionaire [[Stephen M. Ross]], who is one of Benson's larger campaign donors. He was a lobbyist but terminated his lobbying license when Benson launched her gubernatorial campaign. His employer is pushing a controversial AI data center in Saline Township and Friedrichs has said he will recuse himself from any projects before the state should Benson be elected. ''The Michigan Advance'' wrote that "the connection raised concerns about whether Benson could be impartial and free from influence given that Related Digital has such a large business interest before the state."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Solis|first=Ben|date=2025-11-14|title=Benson’s husband to recuse from state work on controversial data center if she is elected governor |url=https://michiganadvance.com/2025/11/14/bensons-husband-to-recuse-from-state-work-on-controversial-data-center-if-she-is-elected-governor/|access-date=2025-11-27|website=Michigan Advance|language=en-US}}</ref>

Benson is a runner and has completed more than 30 marathons.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schuster|first=Simon D.|date=2025-05-06|title=What's a 'purposeful warrior' anyway? Takeaways from Jocelyn Benson's new book|url=https://bridgemi.com/michigan-government/whats-purposeful-warrior-anyway-takeaways-jocelyn-bensons-new-book/|access-date=2025-11-21|website=Bridge Michigan|language=en-US}}</ref> In 2016, she completed her second [[Boston Marathon]] and became one of a handful of women in history to finish the race while eight months pregnant.<ref>{{cite news |date=April 18, 2016|title=Wayne Law Dean Completes Boston Marathon While Pregnant|url=https://www.detroitnews.com/story/sports/2016/04/18/wayne-law-dean-completes-boston-marathon-pregnant/83208496/|access-date=August 31, 2018|newspaper=Detroit News}}</ref>

